  "preamble": "The palatalized stop [dʲ] losing its closure and becoming the palatal glide [j]. The clearest case is the reflex of the Proto-Slavic palatalized stop *ď (from *dj) in the western South Slavic area: Slovene, Kajkavian and Chakavian have j where Shtokavian has đ [dʑ], Bulgarian žd and Russian ž (Proto-Slavic *meďa 'border' > Slovene, Kajkavian meja, Shtokavian međa).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Lenition, Approximantization",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Slovene",
      "languageFamily": "South Slavic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Proto-Slavic *meďa 'border'",
          "to": "méja",
          "note": "Contrast Serbo-Croatian mèđa, Russian межа́, Old Church Slavonic межда."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Slavic *saďa 'soot'",
          "to": "sája",
          "note": "Contrast Serbo-Croatian sađa, Russian са́жа."
        },
        {
          "from": "Proto-Slavic *pręďa 'yarn'",
          "to": "preja [prēja]",
          "note": "Contrast Bulgarian пре́жда, Russian пря́жа."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
